1: # P3K.37. Proved: the actual shorter OS49 scalar loop and all fans
2: 
3: Root, blind_control and checks independently passed all six proof sections on
4: 2026-11-17. The pre-promotion working text is preserved in the audit records.
5: The following exact witnesses are additional direct arithmetic of the proved
6: formula, with their actual fan-basis maps supplied below.
7: 
8: Keep the exact source, component line, cap and fans of F26/F29/K34:
9: \(M=O(G-O)\), suspension by \(-3G\), source \(I_2\) at
10: \(q=1/t=0\), cap order \(m=3k\), \(k>0\), norm
11: \(n=\pm1\), and arbitrary fixed delta character \(d\).
12: The already constructed parameter is \(\lambda=r^{3k}\).
13: The construction below produces an actual family over
14: \[
15:  w=r^3,\qquad \lambda=w^k.                                      \tag{OS.1}
16: \]
17: Its initial positive-diagonal closed Cox pair is
18: \[
19:  (k_{\rm Jin},\ell_{\rm Jin})=(-2k,-8k),\qquad
20:  (m_{\rm rel},n_{\rm rel},s)=(2k,0,0).
21:                                                                     \tag{OS.2}
22: \]
23: For every actual allowed fan, with the physical delta cocharacter norm
24: \(S=a^2-ab+b^2\), its positive-loop class is
25: \[
26:  [\Sigma_{\Phi,T}]=\frac{k}{4}(S-1)(S-3)\pmod {28}.              \tag{OS.3}
27: \]
28: These are conclusions of the actual quotient and framed covering maps below,
29: not a division of a covered homotopy-sphere class.
30: 
31: ## 1. The actual meromorphic +G lift and its component norm
32: 
33: Let \(B_0\) be the actual contracting line lift over \(-3G\), before
34: its scalar parameter is inserted, so \(B_\lambda=\lambda B_0\).
35: The target-point linearization has divisor \(C_0\); as a map of
36: source-point line fibres, \(B_0\) has its zero on source \(C_1\),
37: which is mapped to \(C_0\). This is the exact graph calculation in F31.
38: Consequently \(B_0^{-1}\), over \(+3G\), has its simple pole on
39: source \(C_0\).
40: 
41: There is an actual rational lift \(U\) over \(+G\). In the rational
42: section frame of \(M=O(G-O)\), the function
43: \[
44:  f=\frac1{x-x(G)},\qquad
45:  \operatorname{div}(f)=2O-G-(-G)
46:        =t_G^*(G-O)-(G-O)
47: \]
48: defines the isomorphism \(t_G^*M\to M\); its inverse gives \(U\).
49: All its remaining zeros and poles are vertical. The source translation
50: extends on the actual relatively minimal regular elliptic source, by the
51: canonical-form graph argument already proved in K22.
52: 
53: At the I2 cusp use F31's literal line graph
54: \(h_j=\lceil j/2\rceil\), \(h_{j+2}=h_j+1\).
55: The forward +G source shift is \(j\mapsto j+1\). After multiplying
56: \(U\) by an integral base monomial, its line multiplier has valuation
57: zero. The two actual graph defects are
58: \[
59:  h_j-h_{j+1}=-1\quad(j\text{ even}),\qquad
60:  h_j-h_{j+1}=0\quad(j\text{ odd}).                              \tag{OS.4}
61: \]
62: Thus the pole divisor is \(-C_0\). This includes both node charts,
63: because their endpoint graph defects determine the two fibre-coordinate
64: monomials. The third translated norm has divisor \(-2C_0-C_1\).
65: The maps \(B_0^{-1}\) and \(U^3\) cover the same translation,
66: so their ratio is a rational base function
67: \[
68:  \rho=B_0^{-1}/U^3,\qquad \operatorname{ord}_{q=0}\rho=1.         \tag{OS.5}
69: \]
70: The ratio is constant on each compact generic elliptic fibre. With another
71: whole-fibre normalization of \(U\), the last order is \(1-3e\),
72: which is the same exact congruence needed below.
73: 
74: In particular the component divisor has not been replaced by a whole fibre.
75: It is precisely the unequal translated coefficients in (OS.4) that produce
76: the whole-fibre ratio in (OS.5).
77: 
78: ## 2. Actual cap root lift and its character
79: 
80: On the actual order-\(m\) good root at IV*, the line is the degree-zero
81: root line with Picard point \(G'\). Its marked central point has order
82: three. In the explicit root \(t=z^3,x=z^4X,y=z^6Y\), it is
83: \((X,Y)=(0,1)\) on \(Y^2=X^3+1\); the deck derivative on the
84: elliptic curve is \(\varrho=\zeta_3^2\). For the order-\(3k\)
85: root the same effective deck acts on the central curve.
86: 
87: The translation by +G' has an invertible line lift \(L\) on this actual
88: root line. One may construct it in the universal flat-line model
89: \((\mathbb C\times\mathbb C^*)/\Lambda\), and then compare it to
90: the rational pulled-back \(U\). Their ratio is a meromorphic base scalar;
91: on the smooth central fibre its only divisor is a whole fibre, so removing
92: that base power gives the extending invertible lift. Translation lifts on
93: this degree-zero line commute, as their universal representatives are a
94: source translation and a constant fibre scalar.
95: 
96: The exact Cartier line has deck weights \(\varrho\) at G',
97: \(\varrho^{-1}\) at O, and 1 at the remaining point of the order-three
98: orbit. Translation by +G' cyclically permutes them. Their successive ratios
99: are all \(\varrho^{-1}=\zeta_3\), giving
100: \[
101:  DLD^{-1}=\zeta_3 L.                                             \tag{OS.6}
102: \]
103: The finite cyclic averaging of K35 removes any nonconstant unit in this
104: commutator; an invariant analytic cube root then fixes the actual norm
105: \(L^3=B_0^{-1}\). The fixed \(d\)-character is scalar and commutes
106: with this map.
107: 
108: Write the global rational lift on the root as
109: \(U=z^e a(z)L\). Global deck invariance and the target-scalar identity
110: \(D m_f D^{-1}=m_{f(\zeta_m^{-1}z)}\) give
111: \[
112:  \zeta_m^e=\zeta_3,\qquad a(\zeta_mz)=a(z),\qquad
113:  \operatorname{ord}_{t=0}\rho=-\frac{3e}{m}\equiv-1\pmod3.
114:                                                                     \tag{OS.7}
115: \]
116: At the retained type-II fibre, and at every other ordinary fibre, the
117: irreducible vertical divisor of \(U\) is an integral whole-fibre divisor;
118: the order of \(\rho\) is therefore a multiple of three. No cap or line
119: character has been dropped in these calculations.
120: 
121: ## 3. The global rational endpoint
122: 
123: K34's one-r-turn ordinary CLOSING map is multiplication by
124: \(h_0=t^n=q^{-n}\). Its arrow is from the continued endpoint to the
125: initial chart. Equations (OS.5)--(OS.7) prove that
126: \[
127:                     h_0\rho^{-2n}
128: \]
129: has divisor divisible by three at every source fibre. At the I2 cusp its
130: order is \(-3n\) modulo multiples of three; at IV* its order is
131: \(n+2n=3n\) modulo three; elsewhere it is divisible by three.
132: Factor the rational function on \(\mathbb P^1\), and fix a cube root
133: of its nonzero constant. This gives an actual rational function
134: \[
135:                     g_0^3=h_0\rho^{-2n}.                         \tag{OS.8}
136: \]
137: The proposed ordinary closing map is
138: \[
139:  C_r=r^{2nk}g_0(t)U^{-2n}: X_{\zeta_3r}\longrightarrow X_r.
140:                                                                     \tag{OS.9}
141: \]
142: Its inverse is the forward parameter deck. At an ordinary fibre its base
143: power cancels the whole-fibre power of \(U^{-2n}\), so it is an
144: invertible map of the actual line-complement quotients. The exact maps
145: commute with \(B_\lambda\); the scalar \(\lambda\) is unchanged
146: under \(r\mapsto\zeta_3r\).
147: 
148: On the IV* root, inserting \(U=z^eaL\) and (OS.7) leaves the precise
149: base power \(z^{mn/3}\) times an invariant unit and \(L^{-2n}\).
150: This is the power used in the cap comparison below, not a discarded pole.
151: 
152: ## 4. The typed finite-cap commuting square
153: 
154: Retain F26/F29's physical convention \(V=e^{2\pi i\zeta_2}\).
155: Its root action is
156: \[
157:  A_r=r^n A_0,
158: \]
159: because the actual cap period is
160: \(\ell=n(e_\gamma-e_u)+d\delta\) in F26's homology marking and the
161: gamma period has line multiplier \(\lambda=r^m\).
162: The displayed plus-log map is ordinary to cap:
163: \[
164:  \Phi_r(z,s)=z+\frac{\log s}{2\pi i}\Pi_r(s)\ell.
165: \]
166: Under \(r\mapsto\zeta_3r\), its parameter difference is
167: \((mn/3)\log s\,\delta/(2\pi i)\). It is exactly the positive
168: power left in (OS.9). Thus the actual punctured maps satisfy
169: \[
170:  \Phi_r C_r=C_{\rm cap}\Phi_{\zeta_3r},\qquad
171:  C_{\rm cap}=\text{invariant unit}\cdot L^{-2n}.                 \tag{OS.10}
172: \]
173: This bounded cap map extends across the root fibre. Its conjugation is
174: \[
175:  C_{\rm cap} A_{\zeta_3r} C_{\rm cap}^{-1}
176:    =\zeta_3^n\,(\zeta_3)^{2n} A_r=A_r.                         \tag{OS.11}
177: \]
178: It therefore descends to the exact cap, with the original \(d\)-character.
179: The opposite direction would reverse both the parameter deck and this
180: conjugation; (OS.10) specifies the actual direction used here.
181: 
182: ## 5. The cusp charts, cyclic quotient and identical Cox origin
183: 
184: Use K34's actual periods
185: \[
186:  u=(2s,s)+U(q),\qquad
187:  \gamma=(-3s,-s+mv)+C(q),\qquad
188:  K=3u+2\gamma=D+(s+2mv)\delta,
189: \]
190: where \(D=3U+2C\), \(v=\log r/(2\pi i)\), and
191: \(s=\log q/(2\pi i)\). The source map of \(U\) has Tate
192: valuation +1 and, after the chosen base normalization, line valuation 0.
193: Hence (OS.9) has tropical vector \((-2n,-n)\). Reindexing by the
194: actual source period \(h^n=(2n,n)\) makes it bounded on every toric
195: chart, not only on the fixed curve. This period preserves every allowed
196: index-one fan and every cell label. Choose K34's logarithms coherently with
197: the actual bounded Tate unit of G: the source unit of \(B_0\) is the
198: inverse cube of that unit, because both are the same marked source translation
199: and their valuations already agree. This specifies the first-coordinate
200: logarithm, including its integral period. Changing a previous logarithm by an
201: integer supplies the literal bounded unit torus loop; K34's quaternion
202: identity carries that loop as a normal rotation constant along the fixed
203: sphere, so its free framing class is unchanged. Retain every line unit as
204: follows. Put \(R=\log\rho/(2\pi i)\), let \(L_U\) be the coherent
205: Tate logarithm of \(U\), and put \(g=\log g_0/(2\pi i)\). The
206: actual norm and rational root equations give, with the constant choices
207: retained,
208: \[
209:  3L_U=-\gamma+mv\delta-R\delta,\qquad
210:  3g=-ns-2nR.
211: \]
212: Consequently the logarithm of \(C_r\), after the actual \(h^n\)
213: reindexing, is literally
214: \[
215:  nu-2nL_U+(2nkv+g)\delta
216:    =\frac{nK}{3}-\frac{ns}{3}\delta
217:    =\frac{nD}{3}+\frac{2nmv}{3}\delta.
218: \]
219: Thus all nonconstant units cancel by their actual norm identity, not by an
220: omitted change of frame. The bounded logarithm is exactly
221: \[
222:  \tau(v)=\frac n3\bigl(D+2mv\delta\bigr).                      \tag{OS.12}
223: \]
224: Changing the cube root in (OS.8) by the actual cube root of unity
225: \(e^{-4\pi i nk/3}\) gives the closing map
226: \(\widetilde C_r=e^{2\pi i\alpha}C_r\),
227: \(\alpha=-2nk/3\). This constant is physical delta.
228: 
229: The already specified K34 origin, with no new framing choice, satisfies
230: \[
231:  \sigma(v)=nvD+nmv(v-1)\delta,
232: \]
233: \[
234:  \sigma(v+1/3)-\sigma(v)=\tau(v)+\alpha\delta,
235: \qquad
236:  \sum_{j=0}^{2}\bigl(\tau(v+j/3)+\alpha\delta\bigr)
237:        =nD+2nmv\delta.                                         \tag{OS.13}
238: \]
239: The threefold ordinary composition is exactly
240: \[
241:  \widetilde C_r\widetilde C_{\zeta_3r}
242:        \widetilde C_{\zeta_3^2r}
243:      =h_0 B_\lambda^{2n};                                      \tag{OS.14}
244: \]
245: the parameter-root phase is \(e^{2\pi i2nk}=1\), and the constant
246: correction has cube one. The period \(B_\lambda^{2n}\) is already
247: divided out in the ordinary family. Thus this is exactly its prescribed
248: one-r-turn closing, with the cap square (OS.10), not just its action on
249: cohomology.
250: 
251: First use these maps over the universal v-plane. Their third iterate is
252: the existing v-to-v+1 family gluing. Quotient first by that gluing, obtaining
253: the already constructed r-family. The inverse of (OS.9) then induces an
254: order-three deck over \(r\mapsto\zeta_3r\). It is free because
255: \(r\ne0\). Its quotient is an actual proper holomorphic w-family with
256: (OS.1). The toric bounded units in (OS.12) act on every Cox character,
257: so this quotient retains all three curves, both triple points, and every
258: allowed fan simultaneously.
259: 
260: In K34's product convention the coordinate translation by \(\sigma\)
261: matches the endpoint-to-initial closing (OS.13). Thus the same Cox product
262: closes on the w-loop, and its pullback is literally the original r-product.
263: The constant correction is physical delta. In K34's actual quaternion frame
264: it is right multiplication by a scalar and fixes the Hopf normal vector.
265: The bounded unit comparison is the same \(q_H\mapsto Lq_HR\),
266: independent of the fixed-sphere point. The decreasing-K reflection and both
267: norm signs remain at both endpoints exactly as in K34; no free normal-frame
268: term is introduced.
269: 
270: ## 6. The actual framed covering and the return
271: 
272: For the initial positive fan, the w-loop now has an actual closed normal
273: framing. Its threefold parameter pullback is the exact K34 loop and framing.
274: The induced mapping-torus covering preserves the physical circle Euler line,
275: the complex orientation and the prescribed spin structures. Ordinary
276: characteristic-number naturality therefore gives
277: \[
278:  3k_{\rm Jin}=-6k,\qquad 3\ell_{\rm Jin}=-24k.
279: \]
280: This proves (OS.2) as equations of integers of the constructed mapping torus.
281: Its index is zero. K31's proved recurrence applies to the simultaneous Cox
282: product just obtained, and substituting these two actual integers gives
283: (OS.3). The formula is integral because the physical cocharacter is primitive,
284: so S is odd and \((S-1)(S-3)\) is divisible by eight.
285: 
286: The executed exact certificate is
287: `code/part3_fourfold/os49_shorter_loop.py`. It retains the homology-to-positive
288: Tate sign, rather than identifying those two u coordinates silently. In the
289: displayed F26 homology basis,
290: \(u_{\rm Tate}=-e_u\), \(K=2e_\gamma-3e_u\), and
291: \[
292:  (A-I)K=-3e_w-\delta,\quad
293:  (B-I)K=3e_u+3e_w,\quad (C-I)K=\delta.
294: \]
295: The integral one-step cochain is explicitly
296: \[
297:  p=\frac{-n\delta-n(A-I)K}{3}=ne_w,\qquad
298:  q=-\frac{n(B-I)K}{3}=-n(e_u+e_w),
299: \]
300: \[
301:  p+Aq=0,\qquad q=(B-I)(ne_u),\qquad
302:  k(1+A+A^2)p=-nk\delta.
303: \]
304: Moreover \(-n\delta-3p=n(A-I)K\) and \(-3q=n(B-I)K\),
305: so the threefold comparison retains the exact integer period primitive.
306: These agree with the literal local periods and the finite-root logarithmic
307: power. The component defects, character/divisor identities, cyclic norm,
308: bounded origins and characteristic substitutions all pass in the same
309: certificate; its JSON and captured output are retained alongside the script.
310: 
311: ## 7. Exact scalar-loop witnesses and the range
312: 
313: For \(k=1\), the new parameter is the original scalar itself:
314: \(\lambda=w\). Take the literal oriented fan basis
315: \[
316:  v=(1,0),\qquad w_{\rm fan}=(-4,1),\qquad
317:  \det(v,w_{\rm fan})=1,\qquad \delta=(0,1)=4v+w_{\rm fan}.
318: \]
319: Thus \(S=13\), and the actual positive scalar loop has
320: \[
321:  [\Sigma_{\Phi,T}]=\frac{(13-1)(13-3)}4=30=2\pmod {28}.
322: \]
323: This example uses the shorter family itself and does not select a cube root
324: of a homotopy-sphere class.
325: 
326: The exact \(k=1\) range is
327: \[
328:                   \{0,2,6,12,14,16,20,26\}.                    \tag{OS.15}
329: \]
330: For an upper bound, the formula depends on odd \(S\) modulo 112. Evaluating
331: those 56 residues gives exactly the set displayed. Every value is attained
332: by the following primitive physical cocharacter pairs:
333: \[
334: \begin{array}{c|r|r}
335:  (a,b)&S=a^2-ab+b^2&[\Sigma]\pmod {28}\\ \hline
336:  (0,1)&1&0\\
337:  (1,4)&13&2\\
338:  (1,3)&7&6\\
339:  (3,14)&163&12\\
340:  (1,6)&31&14\\
341:  (2,5)&19&16\\
342:  (1,10)&91&20\\
343:  (3,7)&37&26
344: \end{array}
345: \]
346: These are actual fan coordinates. For any displayed coprime pair choose
347: integers \(r_0,s_0\) with \(ar_0+bs_0=1\), and set
348: \(v=(b,r_0)\), \(w_{\rm fan}=(-a,s_0)\). Then
349: \(\det(v,w_{\rm fan})=1\) and \(av+bw_{\rm fan}=(0,1)=\delta\).
350: Thus each row supplies the required actual unimodular fan, with no
351: realizability inference from an unattained residue.
352: 
353: All values for every \(k\) are even: with \(S=2j+1\), (OS.3) is
354: \(kj(j-1)\). Conversely the fixed \(S=13\) fan gives \(2k\) modulo28.
355: As the actual cap order \(m=3k\) varies through the already constructed
356: positive integers, these shorter scalar families therefore realize exactly
357: the fourteen even classes. The proof of source-translation endpoint
358: minimality is retained separately with its explicit scope; it is not used
359: to infer these realized classes.
